verdicts.reasoning:
Verified end-to-end, not just by reading. (1) serialport 4.10.0 (Cargo.lock) TTYPort::read -> posix/poll.rs wait_fd returns io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::TimedOut, "Operation timed out") when poll expires (poll.rs:34-38); open_port sets .timeout(200ms) (worktree serial.rs:75). (2) std BufRead::read_line keeps already-read bytes in the String on Err; proved with a standalone rustc repro in the scratchpad (repro/repro.rs) using a mock Read yielding "ack {" then Err(TimedOut) then ""id":1}
": the loop exactly as written in serial.rs (line.clear() before every read_line, `{}` on TimedOut) emits only ""id":1}
"; moving the clear to after a completed line emits the full "ack {"id":1}
". (3) Reader loop in serial.rs:249-267 (HEAD :244-262) matches the claim verbatim: `line.clear(); match buf.read_line(&mut line) { Ok(0)=>break, Ok(_)=>parse, Err(e) if TimedOut|Interrupted|WouldBlock => {}, Err(_)=>break }`. (4) parse_device_msg (protocol.rs:88-94) is serde_json::from_str(..).ok(), so the orphaned tail is dropped silently with no log. (5) Consequence chain holds: missing ack -> protocol.rs:580 "no ack for action … — outcome UNKNOWN (not retried)" / LinkError::Uncertain; link_caps notes sendOutcomeUnknown. (6) Not covered by tests: serial.rs tests are reconnect-drops-queue, oversize-line (new, uncommitted), expired-entry, enotty-fallback x2, pty-open/shutdown; all local paths use the pty File fallback which has no read timeout, so this branch never runs under test. Not listed in CHANGELOG/acceptance-evidence/gap-matrix. (7) Working-tree diff to serial.rs only adds MAX_LINE_BYTES/check_line_size near the top (+5 lines); the reader loop is unchanged, hence line drift 246->251, not a fix. Caveat on realism (why severity stays low, not higher): the reference firmware's sendLine is a single Serial.println(line) (esp32-companion.ino:479-482) and the UART FIFO drains in hardware, so DHT/pulseIn stalls cannot split a line on the reference device; the trigger requires a third-party protocol-v1 device writing a line in pieces or a host USB-serial stall >200ms mid-line. Still a genuine host-side robustness defect on the real-hardware path. Note for the fix: retaining `line` across timeouts also needs an explicit length cap (device that never sends '
'), since the current per-200ms clear is what accidentally bounds it today.
